        <!-- SECTION 9: Contemporary Photography -->
        <div class="section-heading-with-line">
          <h2>Cowboy Art in Contemporary Photography</h2>
        </div>

        <p>Today, Western cowboy art is not limited to painting or sculpture. Photography has become an increasingly important part of the <a href="/Blog/what-is-western-fine-art" style="color:#8b5a2b; text-decoration:underline;">Western fine art</a> conversation.</p>

        <p>When practiced as fine art, cowboy photography follows the same principles that guided earlier painters:</p>
        <ul style="list-style:disc; padding-left:1.5rem; margin:1rem 0 1.5rem;">
          <li style="margin-bottom:0.5rem;">Intentional composition</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom:0.5rem;">Narrative presence</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom:0.5rem;">Emotional restraint</li>
          <li>Authorial vision</li>
        </ul>

        <p>The goal is not simply to record what a cowboy looks like. It is to interpret what the figure represents.</p>

        <p>Contemporary photographic work often focuses on:</p>
        <ul style="list-style:disc; padding-left:1.5rem; margin:1rem 0 1.5rem;">
          <li style="margin-bottom:0.5rem;">Character over stereotype</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom:0.5rem;">Atmosphere over spectacle</li>
          <li>Story over symbol</li>
        </ul>

        <p>In this way, modern cowboy photography becomes part of the same artistic lineage—extending the Western canon into new mediums while preserving its narrative core.</p>

        <!-- SECTION 10: Explore CTAs -->
        <div class="section-heading-with-line">
          <h2>Explore Western Cowboy Art at K4 Studios</h2>
